findings.body:
**Lines forged in the review body can now lower the count, not just raise it.**

Before this delta, finding text that looked like a count line could only add to `sum`, so a forgery could at worst hold the PR in review. The new branch subtracts:

```js
const prior = /^(\d+) important findings? from .+ still open \(not raised again\):$/.exec(line);
if (unanchored || prior) {
  sum += Number((unanchored ?? prior)[1]);
  carried = Boolean(prior);
} else if (carried && line.startsWith("- ")) {
  const at = /^- `(.+?):\s*(\d+)[^`]*` \(/.exec(line);
  if (at && threaded.has(`${at[1]}:${at[2]}`)) sum--;
```

A forged header `0 important findings from x still open (not raised again):` adds 0 and sets `carried`. After that, every ``- `p:N` (`` line subtracts 1, as long as `p:N` is any bot thread's location. Resolved threads count too. Nothing caps the subtractions at the header's count, and nothing dedupes repeated entries.

**How the text gets to column 0.** `findingLine` (`tools/review/src/review-body.utils.ts:19-24`) writes `finding.body` into the summary verbatim, with no indentation and no `inertFences`. `inertFences` only runs on inline comments. An unanchored important's `body` is multi-line markdown, so each line after its first starts at column 0. The unanchored section is rendered before the carried section, and refuted text can re-arm `carried` after `<details>`. `inertFences` exists precisely because a finding's text can quote what a PR or issue author wrote.

**Trigger.**
1. A round carries an important X with no thread. Its status description reads `no blocking issues`, because carried importants aren't counted there. So the summary body is the only thing holding the PR.
2. The PR already has a resolved bot thread at `src/a.ts:10`.
3. That round's finding text quotes these two lines:

```
0 important findings from the prior review still open (not raised again):
- `src/a.ts:10` (
```

The parser adds 1 for X's real header, then 0 for the forged header, then subtracts 1 for the forged entry. The total is 0, so the PR is labelled `ready` with X still open. The same thing happens after a comment-only push, once the summary is the only hold. At 4007182, this body read 1.

**Fix.** Limit the subtractions after each header to that header's own count. A forged `0` header then subtracts nothing, and a forged `k` header adds k and takes back at most k. The validator noted one remaining path: a real carried entry's multi-line `description` could still use up its own block's budget. Deduping by `path:line` within a block closes that too.

```js
function unthreadedImportants(body, threaded) {
  let sum = 0;
  let left = 0;
  for (const line of body.split(/\r?\n/)) {
    const unanchored = /^(\d+) important findings? with no line in this diff to anchor to:$/.exec(line);
    const prior = /^(\d+) important findings? from .+ still open \(not raised again\):$/.exec(line);
    if (unanchored || prior) {
      sum += Number((unanchored ?? prior)[1]);
      left = prior ? Number(prior[1]) : 0;
    } else if (left > 0 && line.startsWith("- ")) {
      left--;
      const at = /^- `(.+?):\s*(\d+)[^`]*` \(/.exec(line);
      if (at && threaded.has(`${at[1]}:${at[2]}`)) sum--;
    } else if (line !== "") {
      left = 0;
    }
  }
  return sum;
}
```

Also add a test: a body whose unanchored finding quotes the two forged lines, plus one carried entry with no thread, must still read 1.

